1
00:00:01,401 --> 00:00:03,199
♪♪

2
00:02:07,494 --> 00:02:11,295
Dad, just get back
in your car and leave.

3
00:02:12,499 --> 00:02:14,195
Or what?

4
00:02:17,537 --> 00:02:19,301
You stay away from my mother.

5
00:02:19,439 --> 00:02:21,601
Get out of my way, Dan.

6
00:02:24,277 --> 00:02:26,303
I'm warning you!

7
00:02:26,446 --> 00:02:28,210
Don't go in there.

8
00:02:50,604 --> 00:02:53,836
Anything you say can and will be
used against you in a court of law.

9
00:02:53,974 --> 00:02:56,653
If you cannot afford an attorney,
the court will appoint one for you.

10
00:02:56,677 --> 00:02:58,009
Oh, Danny.

11
00:02:58,145 --> 00:03:00,239
Do you understand what
I just told you, Danny?

12
00:03:00,380 --> 00:03:01,848
I understand.

13
00:03:01,982 --> 00:03:04,178
Please don't take
my boy. Please.

14
00:03:04,317 --> 00:03:05,757
I'm sorry, ma'am.
Please stand aside.

15
00:03:05,819 --> 00:03:08,311
Mom, it's OK, it's all
right. You stay here.

16
00:03:13,226 --> 00:03:15,195
Grandma.

17
00:03:27,441 --> 00:03:30,343
- Hey, Ben?
- Yeah.

18
00:03:30,477 --> 00:03:31,877
I brought you that book...

19
00:03:32,012 --> 00:03:33,389
<i>Better Health Through
Fruits and Vegetables.</i>

20
00:03:33,413 --> 00:03:34,540
Oh, good.

21
00:03:36,983 --> 00:03:39,418
- You're not going to eat that.
- I bet I can.

22
00:03:39,553 --> 00:03:42,045
What about <i>Health Through
Fruits and Vegetables?</i>

23
00:03:42,189 --> 00:03:43,189
Well...

24
00:03:43,290 --> 00:03:46,692
Chicken dipped in batter and
drenched in oil isn't healthy.

25
00:03:46,827 --> 00:03:49,319
I know, but I get
to have some fun.

26
00:03:49,463 --> 00:03:52,194
Every time I turn on the
TV, I see a news report

27
00:03:52,332 --> 00:03:55,200
about how something I've
eaten all my life is going to kill me

28
00:03:55,335 --> 00:03:58,999
and the five or six things
that are still OK are dangerous

29
00:03:59,139 --> 00:04:02,371
because of the stuff they spray
on it or pour on it or wrap it in.

30
00:04:02,509 --> 00:04:04,603
The air's no good.
The water's no good.

31
00:04:04,745 --> 00:04:08,944
The ozone is going like 60-cent gasoline,
so I'll worry about it all tomorrow.

32
00:04:09,082 --> 00:04:10,414
What's that?

33
00:04:10,550 --> 00:04:11,813
It's going to be dumplings.

34
00:04:11,952 --> 00:04:13,818
You can't eat dumplings.

35
00:04:13,954 --> 00:04:15,217
I bet I can.

36
00:04:15,355 --> 00:04:17,620
Not without gravy.

37
00:04:18,825 --> 00:04:20,851
I make terrific gravy.

38
00:04:20,994 --> 00:04:22,929
- You're invited.
- Hmm.

39
00:04:24,731 --> 00:04:27,291
- Would you get that?
- Sure.

40
00:04:28,602 --> 00:04:30,161
Hello?

41
00:04:30,303 --> 00:04:32,670
Christine? Slow down.

42
00:04:35,542 --> 00:04:37,841
Christine, that's terrible.

43
00:04:42,582 --> 00:04:44,278
How is my mom?

44
00:04:45,919 --> 00:04:48,218
She's hanging in there, Danny.

45
00:04:50,824 --> 00:04:52,588
Is somebody with her?

46
00:04:52,726 --> 00:04:54,217
She shouldn't be alone.

47
00:04:58,365 --> 00:05:00,425
Your grandmother is with her.

48
00:05:02,435 --> 00:05:03,801
Danny...

49
00:05:05,438 --> 00:05:08,704
your mom told me
that you shot your dad

50
00:05:08,842 --> 00:05:11,676
because you thought he was
going inside the house to kill her.

51
00:05:11,812 --> 00:05:15,340
No, no, no. He said that
he was going to kill her.

52
00:05:15,482 --> 00:05:17,314
I knew that he meant that.

53
00:05:17,450 --> 00:05:22,354
Sometimes people threaten
terrible things when they're angry,

54
00:05:22,489 --> 00:05:25,301
but later on, when they calm down.
They never really meant what they said.

55
00:05:25,325 --> 00:05:28,090
Not my dad.

56
00:05:28,228 --> 00:05:30,561
If he said he was going
to do something, he did it.

57
00:05:30,697 --> 00:05:32,461
Like what?

58
00:05:32,599 --> 00:05:35,125
What kinds of things would
he threaten and then do?

59
00:05:37,370 --> 00:05:42,775
My dad wanted dinner on
the table every night at 6 P.M.

60
00:05:42,909 --> 00:05:47,711
He told my mom that meant
6:00... Not 6:05 or 6:10...

61
00:05:47,848 --> 00:05:50,750
And the next time dinner
wasn't ready at 6:00...

62
00:05:52,686 --> 00:05:55,121
he'd bash her face in.

63
00:05:59,426 --> 00:06:01,827
Your father physically
abused your mother?

64
00:06:03,630 --> 00:06:05,121
He broke her leg.

65
00:06:07,801 --> 00:06:10,703
Your mother told me that she
slipped and fell down the stairs.

66
00:06:12,272 --> 00:06:14,332
No. He pushed her.

67
00:06:15,909 --> 00:06:19,437
That's... That's
why they separated.

68
00:06:19,579 --> 00:06:25,313
He'd hit her or...
throw things at her.

69
00:06:26,686 --> 00:06:29,713
He used to lock
her in the closet.

70
00:06:31,524 --> 00:06:35,757
I remember lying
in bed at night,

71
00:06:35,896 --> 00:06:38,229
hearing her scream and cry.

72
00:06:41,034 --> 00:06:43,594
I remember one time he hit her

73
00:06:43,737 --> 00:06:47,333
because she threw out the
newspaper before he could read it.

74
00:06:50,010 --> 00:06:52,479
Your mom never
told me any of this.

75
00:06:52,612 --> 00:06:54,376
She didn't tell anybody.

76
00:06:54,514 --> 00:06:58,007
She wanted
everybody to think that...

77
00:06:58,151 --> 00:07:00,120
our family was normal.

78
00:07:00,253 --> 00:07:02,518
She even made excuses for him.

79
00:07:04,624 --> 00:07:06,957
How long had all
this been going on?

80
00:07:08,228 --> 00:07:10,026
Forever.

81
00:07:10,163 --> 00:07:15,067
The last few months,
it just got worse.

82
00:07:15,201 --> 00:07:17,670
He got madder and madder.

83
00:07:21,808 --> 00:07:23,800
I had to shoot him.

84
00:07:26,680 --> 00:07:29,775
The problem is, Danny,
you didn't have to shoot him.

85
00:07:31,551 --> 00:07:33,663
If you thought your father
was going to kill your mother,

86
00:07:33,687 --> 00:07:35,656
why didn't you get
her out of the house?

87
00:07:35,789 --> 00:07:38,156
I tried. She wouldn't go.

88
00:07:38,291 --> 00:07:40,658
My dad wouldn't let her work,
she had no money of her own.

89
00:07:40,794 --> 00:07:42,023
Where was she supposed to go?

90
00:07:42,162 --> 00:07:44,722
- You could have called the police.
- I did.

91
00:07:44,864 --> 00:07:47,766
The police asked me
if he was standing there

92
00:07:47,901 --> 00:07:50,379
waving a gun or anything like that...
Was there any immediate danger?

93
00:07:50,403 --> 00:07:51,598
I said no.

94
00:07:51,738 --> 00:07:55,539
They said that they needed more
reason than a threat to arrest him,

95
00:07:55,675 --> 00:07:57,405
and that if my mom
wanted him to stay away,

96
00:07:57,544 --> 00:07:59,672
she'd have to file
a restraining order.

97
00:08:02,115 --> 00:08:04,380
Yeah, that's standard procedure.

98
00:08:07,921 --> 00:08:09,514
Can you help me?

99
00:08:12,292 --> 00:08:13,885
I'll sure try.

100
00:08:17,030 --> 00:08:19,898
Um, will you do me a favor?

101
00:08:20,033 --> 00:08:21,592
If I can.

102
00:08:24,504 --> 00:08:28,703
Will you tell my
grandmother I'm sorry?

103
00:08:33,213 --> 00:08:36,115
First, I'm going to tell Ben
everything you told me.

104
00:08:37,450 --> 00:08:39,885
And then we'll see about
getting the charges reduced.

105
00:08:40,020 --> 00:08:43,013
No prosecutor would
push for first degree murder

106
00:08:43,156 --> 00:08:45,819
if he heard what you just
told me about your dad.

107
00:08:50,030 --> 00:08:52,158
Mr. Connolly, were you surprised

108
00:08:52,298 --> 00:08:54,767
the jury reached a
guilty verdict this quickly?

109
00:08:54,901 --> 00:08:57,336
Surprised. Delighted.

110
00:08:57,470 --> 00:08:59,701
Hungry. Sleepy. That just
about covers all my emotions.

111
00:08:59,839 --> 00:09:03,207
Ben, I wish you had seen
me in that courtroom today.

112
00:09:03,343 --> 00:09:04,902
- That good, huh?
- Better.

113
00:09:05,045 --> 00:09:06,325
I just threw a shutout in there.

114
00:09:06,446 --> 00:09:08,608
- Hey.
- Excuse me.

115
00:09:08,748 --> 00:09:13,015
Um, I hear you've been
assigned the Danny Hayes case.

116
00:09:13,153 --> 00:09:14,644
That's a fact.

117
00:09:14,788 --> 00:09:16,689
You're not thinking of
defending him, are you?

118
00:09:16,823 --> 00:09:17,950
I'm considering it.

119
00:09:18,091 --> 00:09:19,423
I'm surprised.

120
00:09:19,559 --> 00:09:22,290
You generally don't defend
people who confess to murder.

121
00:09:22,429 --> 00:09:24,796
Ordinarily I don't, but, uh...

122
00:09:24,931 --> 00:09:28,663
I don't think this is
a murder one case.

123
00:09:29,869 --> 00:09:31,303
You're kidding.

124
00:09:31,438 --> 00:09:34,567
Frank Hayes had a
history of violent behavior.

125
00:09:34,707 --> 00:09:35,936
He was a wife-beater.

126
00:09:36,076 --> 00:09:37,738
That wasn't in
the police report.

127
00:09:37,877 --> 00:09:39,675
Well, his wife didn't report it.

128
00:09:39,813 --> 00:09:43,909
But Frank Hayes told his
wife that he would kill her

129
00:09:44,050 --> 00:09:46,918
if he found her in
the house that night.

130
00:09:47,053 --> 00:09:49,215
And given his history
of violent behavior,

131
00:09:49,355 --> 00:09:50,584
Danny believed him.

132
00:09:50,723 --> 00:09:53,192
Danny was just
defending his mother.

133
00:09:53,326 --> 00:09:56,990
And that gave him license to
just shoot his father in cold blood?

134
00:09:57,130 --> 00:09:59,122
- I didn't...
- Ben.

135
00:09:59,265 --> 00:10:01,291
I'll grant you I'm no
Harvard Law graduate,

136
00:10:01,434 --> 00:10:02,959
but where I went to school,

137
00:10:03,103 --> 00:10:07,040
if a kid sits hiding in a garage for
two hours with a gun in his hand,

138
00:10:07,173 --> 00:10:10,803
then shoots his unarmed father
the minute he gets out of his car,

139
00:10:10,944 --> 00:10:12,970
well, that's murder
one in a very big way.

140
00:10:13,113 --> 00:10:16,515
He didn't shoot him the
minute he stepped out of his car.

141
00:10:16,649 --> 00:10:19,778
He was trying to get him
not to go in the house.

142
00:10:19,919 --> 00:10:24,254
He was protecting his mother,
not going to shoot his father.

143
00:10:24,390 --> 00:10:26,416
But he did shoot his father.

144
00:10:26,559 --> 00:10:29,393
I'm surprised at you.

145
00:10:29,529 --> 00:10:30,997
This kid handed you a line,

146
00:10:31,131 --> 00:10:33,066
and you swallowed it
hook, line, and sinker.

147
00:10:33,199 --> 00:10:36,658
I believe what Danny told me.

148
00:10:36,803 --> 00:10:41,366
Ben, your client was
lying in wait for his victim.

149
00:10:41,508 --> 00:10:44,603
You know that constitutes
extraordinary circumstances.

150
00:10:44,744 --> 00:10:48,010
It's wrong to put this boy
on trial for murder one.

151
00:10:49,816 --> 00:10:52,047
Well, we'll see
what a jury thinks.

152
00:10:58,491 --> 00:11:00,392
See you in court, Counselor.

153
00:11:05,064 --> 00:11:06,896
Jackass.

154
00:11:12,172 --> 00:11:15,631
Mr. Connolly, are you ready
to give your opening statement?

155
00:11:15,775 --> 00:11:17,368
I am, Your Honor.

156
00:11:17,510 --> 00:11:21,311
It is June 10th of this
year, ladies and gentlemen.

157
00:11:24,050 --> 00:11:28,044
It is the last few minutes
of Frank Hayes' life.

158
00:11:32,759 --> 00:11:34,193
As he exits his car

159
00:11:34,327 --> 00:11:38,628
and he walks the five or seven feet
toward the door leading to his house...

160
00:11:38,765 --> 00:11:42,361
Even as his hand
reaches for the knob...

161
00:11:43,870 --> 00:11:47,204
gunshots shatter
the evening calm

162
00:11:47,340 --> 00:11:49,241
and enter Frank Hayes' back,

163
00:11:49,375 --> 00:11:53,403
to the left of the spine,
and lodge in his heart.

164
00:11:57,016 --> 00:12:01,613
The evidence in this case will
show, beyond a reasonable doubt...

165
00:12:03,990 --> 00:12:07,950
that some person very close to
Frank Hayes had come to hate him...

166
00:12:09,529 --> 00:12:13,523
was waiting in the
garage for him to arrive,

167
00:12:13,666 --> 00:12:16,727
took aim with a
high-powered handgun...

168
00:12:18,671 --> 00:12:22,699
and, without any justification
or provocation whatsoever,

169
00:12:22,842 --> 00:12:27,212
killed him in cold, cold blood.

170
00:12:29,315 --> 00:12:34,549
A plotted, planned and premeditated
murder of a human being.

171
00:12:38,524 --> 00:12:43,360
That person is seated
in this courtroom...

172
00:12:46,165 --> 00:12:49,158
at that table, in that chair.

173
00:12:51,638 --> 00:12:54,938
That person's name
is Daniel Hayes.

174
00:12:55,074 --> 00:12:57,305
He is the son of Frank Hayes,

175
00:12:57,443 --> 00:13:00,538
and he is the
defendant in this case.

176
00:13:03,349 --> 00:13:07,445
Ladies and gentlemen, the
evidence you will hear speaks for itself.

177
00:13:08,855 --> 00:13:12,690
The state asks only that
you listen to that evidence...

178
00:13:14,394 --> 00:13:17,796
and you give us your verdict
in accordance with the law.

179
00:13:27,340 --> 00:13:29,104
Mr. Matlock?

180
00:13:34,781 --> 00:13:38,343
That was a very
convincing speech.

181
00:13:40,687 --> 00:13:46,627
So, why don't we just convict
Danny Hayes of murder right now

182
00:13:46,759 --> 00:13:49,319
and send him to prison
for the rest of his life?

183
00:13:49,462 --> 00:13:53,126
Of course, you would
be disregarding the law,

184
00:13:53,266 --> 00:13:55,895
but that's what my
distinguished colleague

185
00:13:56,035 --> 00:13:58,766
apparently wants you to do.

186
00:13:58,905 --> 00:14:04,242
He is absolutely
right about one thing.

187
00:14:06,045 --> 00:14:08,708
Murder is never justified.

188
00:14:08,848 --> 00:14:13,286
However, we do
have, in this country,

189
00:14:13,419 --> 00:14:19,017
a thing called
"justifiable homicide".

190
00:14:19,158 --> 00:14:22,151
Every state in
the Union allows it.

191
00:14:22,295 --> 00:14:26,858
It's a law that simply means...

192
00:14:26,999 --> 00:14:30,936
in defense of
another human being,

193
00:14:31,070 --> 00:14:36,737
you, you, you, or Danny Hayes,

194
00:14:36,876 --> 00:14:43,248
has the right to keep that
person from being killed.

195
00:14:43,383 --> 00:14:45,943
We don't have to
sit on our hands.

196
00:14:47,920 --> 00:14:51,288
Now, you're going
to hear evidence

197
00:14:51,424 --> 00:14:55,759
that Frank Hayes was
not just an ordinary man

198
00:14:55,895 --> 00:14:58,763
who lost his temper
once in a while.

199
00:14:58,898 --> 00:15:02,096
This was a violent,
intolerant man

200
00:15:02,235 --> 00:15:06,730
who frequently slapped,
beat, abused his wife.

201
00:15:06,873 --> 00:15:10,833
And then, when his
temper reached its peak,

202
00:15:10,977 --> 00:15:17,247
he made a promise that he
would return at a specified time

203
00:15:17,383 --> 00:15:21,445
and take the life of
Danny Hayes' mother.

204
00:15:21,587 --> 00:15:23,852
And Danny believed his father.

205
00:15:23,990 --> 00:15:25,549
He took him at his word.

206
00:15:27,760 --> 00:15:31,390
Frank Hayes was a
wife beater, abuser.

207
00:15:31,531 --> 00:15:33,466
He would say he was
sorry, wouldn't do it again,

208
00:15:33,599 --> 00:15:37,127
and then something would
set off this violent man.

209
00:15:37,270 --> 00:15:42,675
And he said to his wife, "I'm
coming back here tomorrow,

210
00:15:42,809 --> 00:15:47,179
"and if you're still here,
I'm going to kill you."

211
00:15:48,614 --> 00:15:50,947
And Danny believed him.

212
00:15:51,083 --> 00:15:54,576
After you've heard
all the testimony,

213
00:15:54,720 --> 00:16:00,421
I'm going to ask you to put
yourself in Danny's shoes.

214
00:16:00,560 --> 00:16:02,552
Ask yourself...

215
00:16:04,764 --> 00:16:07,700
"Would I defend my mother

216
00:16:07,834 --> 00:16:09,996
"if I were certain

217
00:16:10,136 --> 00:16:13,072
"that, in a matter of seconds,

218
00:16:13,206 --> 00:16:16,108
"her life would be ended?"

219
00:16:20,379 --> 00:16:22,109
Would you?

220
00:16:34,060 --> 00:16:35,653
OK, there you go.

221
00:16:35,795 --> 00:16:37,058
Eat your pie.

222
00:16:37,196 --> 00:16:39,028
Oh, no, thanks. I'm not hungry.

223
00:16:39,165 --> 00:16:40,656
Danny, you have to eat.

224
00:16:40,800 --> 00:16:43,269
I don't want you looking
pale in the courtroom.

225
00:16:49,442 --> 00:16:52,776
I dream about him every night.

226
00:16:54,981 --> 00:16:58,383
I see his face, hear Mom crying.

227
00:16:58,518 --> 00:17:00,749
I'm not surprised.

228
00:17:02,221 --> 00:17:04,213
I'm very worried.

229
00:17:04,357 --> 00:17:07,156
If your mother had just
called the cops once, twice...

230
00:17:07,293 --> 00:17:09,626
Once... it would have helped.

231
00:17:09,762 --> 00:17:12,082
At least your father's abuse
would have been on the record.

232
00:17:12,164 --> 00:17:14,929
If you had left your name when
you called, that would have helped.

233
00:17:15,067 --> 00:17:17,059
Wait, you don't believe me?

234
00:17:17,203 --> 00:17:21,334
My belief isn't necessary
or even important.

235
00:17:22,575 --> 00:17:24,043
Reasonable doubt.

236
00:17:27,413 --> 00:17:30,611
There is no doubt
you shot your father.

237
00:17:34,086 --> 00:17:37,147
We have to make the jury believe

238
00:17:37,290 --> 00:17:41,057
that you felt you had to shoot
him to save another person's life.

239
00:17:44,196 --> 00:17:47,564
Ain't nothing easy.
And this sure ain't.

240
00:17:47,700 --> 00:17:49,168
Eat your pie.

241
00:17:53,239 --> 00:17:54,434
Eat your pie.

242
00:18:00,947 --> 00:18:03,576
Mr. Connolly, would
you like to proceed?

243
00:18:03,716 --> 00:18:04,877
Thank you, Your Honor.

244
00:18:05,017 --> 00:18:06,918
Mr. Blessing...

245
00:18:08,387 --> 00:18:10,583
are you acquainted
with the defendant?

246
00:18:10,723 --> 00:18:12,988
We went to high school together.

247
00:18:13,125 --> 00:18:15,924
Do you recall an incident that
took place about a year ago

248
00:18:16,062 --> 00:18:19,055
between you and the defendant
regarding a gold-plated pen?

249
00:18:19,198 --> 00:18:21,861
Yeah, I'd call it an incident.

250
00:18:22,001 --> 00:18:25,130
Will you elaborate
for the court, please?

251
00:18:25,271 --> 00:18:27,934
Danny accused me
of stealing his pen.

252
00:18:28,074 --> 00:18:29,940
Had you stolen it?

253
00:18:30,076 --> 00:18:32,387
No. He found it later, scrunched
in the back of his car seat.

254
00:18:32,411 --> 00:18:35,472
Mr. Blessing, when Mr. Hayes
suspected you had stolen his pen,

255
00:18:35,615 --> 00:18:39,518
did he tell a teacher or take
you aside and talk to you?

256
00:18:39,652 --> 00:18:41,430
Are you kidding? He
knocked the crap out of me.

257
00:18:41,454 --> 00:18:43,719
He hit you?

258
00:18:43,856 --> 00:18:46,052
He cracked my tooth.

259
00:18:46,192 --> 00:18:48,718
What did you do to
provoke Mr. Hayes' rage?

260
00:18:48,861 --> 00:18:50,762
Nothing. I was in
the locker room.

261
00:18:50,896 --> 00:18:53,576
I was headed for the showers, and
he just came up and blindsided me.

262
00:18:54,533 --> 00:18:57,298
You mean he attacked you
because he believed you had his pen

263
00:18:57,436 --> 00:18:59,928
- and not because he had any proof?
- That's right.

264
00:19:00,072 --> 00:19:03,804
Sounds to me like Daniel
Hayes has a hair-trigger temper.

265
00:19:03,943 --> 00:19:05,775
Nothing further.

266
00:19:08,347 --> 00:19:10,282
Mr. Matlock, your witness.

267
00:19:11,450 --> 00:19:13,749
Mr. Blessing...

268
00:19:13,886 --> 00:19:18,449
uh, have you ever
been in a fistfight before?

269
00:19:18,591 --> 00:19:20,059
Yeah, lots of times.

270
00:19:20,192 --> 00:19:24,061
Oh. Were you ever in a
fistfight with Mr. Hayes before?

271
00:19:25,431 --> 00:19:26,797
Well, yeah.

272
00:19:26,932 --> 00:19:29,094
What was that fight about?

273
00:19:30,436 --> 00:19:32,098
He asked out my girlfriend.

274
00:19:32,238 --> 00:19:36,107
Ohh, he made a
move on your girl.

275
00:19:36,242 --> 00:19:38,939
That must have made you mad.

276
00:19:39,078 --> 00:19:43,140
In that fight, who
threw the first punch?

277
00:19:44,750 --> 00:19:46,719
Who threw the first punch?

278
00:19:46,852 --> 00:19:47,945
I did, but he was asking...

279
00:19:48,087 --> 00:19:49,214
No further questions.

280
00:19:49,355 --> 00:19:50,846
Witness may step down.

281
00:19:54,894 --> 00:19:57,022
You may call your next witness.

282
00:19:59,198 --> 00:20:01,861
The state calls Sam
Parker to the stand.

283
00:20:02,001 --> 00:20:04,197
Mr. Parker, you were
a close, personal friend

284
00:20:04,336 --> 00:20:06,066
of Frank Hayes, were you not?

285
00:20:06,205 --> 00:20:09,266
I knew Frank for 21 years.
He was my best friend.

286
00:20:09,408 --> 00:20:11,639
In all those years
of friendship,

287
00:20:11,777 --> 00:20:14,679
did you ever witness any violent
behavior from Frank Hayes?

288
00:20:14,814 --> 00:20:18,478
- Never.
- See him uncontrollably enraged?

289
00:20:18,617 --> 00:20:21,678
- Be physically abusive to anyone?
- No.

290
00:20:22,822 --> 00:20:24,347
How about to his wife?

291
00:20:24,490 --> 00:20:27,949
No way. Frank was a
really good husband.

292
00:20:28,094 --> 00:20:30,393
How about his
relationship with his son?

293
00:20:30,529 --> 00:20:32,930
- Did he ever talk to you about it?
- Sometimes.

294
00:20:33,065 --> 00:20:36,627
He couldn't understand why
Danny hated him so much.

295
00:20:38,504 --> 00:20:42,441
Do you recall the night
before Frank Hayes' death?

296
00:20:42,575 --> 00:20:45,670
Yes. I spent part of
the evening with him.

297
00:20:45,811 --> 00:20:48,280
We had dinner, and then
I drove him to his house.

298
00:20:48,414 --> 00:20:51,441
Now, in your own words,
I'd like you to explain

299
00:20:51,584 --> 00:20:54,816
the events of that evening, as
best you can remember them.

300
00:20:54,954 --> 00:20:57,947
After Frank and
Christine had separated,

301
00:20:58,090 --> 00:21:00,719
Frank and I started getting
together once a week for dinner.

302
00:21:00,860 --> 00:21:04,763
Anyway, we were sitting
in this cafe that night,

303
00:21:04,897 --> 00:21:08,129
and Frank started telling me
how much he missed Christine.

304
00:21:08,267 --> 00:21:11,135
Finally, I told him
to stop telling me this

305
00:21:11,270 --> 00:21:13,569
and get over and tell her.

306
00:21:16,776 --> 00:21:18,142
Chris?

307
00:21:19,945 --> 00:21:21,504
Chris, it's Frank.

308
00:21:23,149 --> 00:21:25,550
<i>Christine was wearing
a real sexy dress.</i>

309
00:21:25,684 --> 00:21:27,152
<i>You could see she was mad</i>

310
00:21:27,286 --> 00:21:29,118
<i>because Frank showed
up without calling.</i>

311
00:21:29,255 --> 00:21:30,484
Why didn't you call?

312
00:21:30,623 --> 00:21:32,649
I've missed you, Chris.

313
00:21:34,293 --> 00:21:37,627
Ask Sam here. You're
all I ever talk about.

314
00:21:37,763 --> 00:21:40,232
You should have
called first, Frank.

315
00:21:41,634 --> 00:21:43,364
Is everything okay, Chris?

316
00:21:44,637 --> 00:21:46,071
Who are you?

317
00:21:46,205 --> 00:21:48,333
Jack, this is my husband.

318
00:21:48,474 --> 00:21:50,466
What's this guy
doing in my house?

319
00:21:50,609 --> 00:21:52,771
Well, that's none of
your business, Frank.

320
00:21:52,912 --> 00:21:55,143
You're the one who said
we should see other people.

321
00:21:55,281 --> 00:21:56,681
What?

322
00:21:56,816 --> 00:21:58,256
What the hell are
you talking about?

323
00:21:58,384 --> 00:22:00,717
<i>About then, Danny
came out of his room.</i>

324
00:22:01,887 --> 00:22:03,515
I think maybe I'd better go.

325
00:22:03,656 --> 00:22:05,887
I'll call you later.

326
00:22:14,300 --> 00:22:16,428
- Where are you going?
- To bed.

327
00:22:16,569 --> 00:22:18,094
Chris, we've got to talk.

328
00:22:18,237 --> 00:22:20,832
<i>Frank started upstairs
to talk to Christine,</i>

329
00:22:20,973 --> 00:22:22,942
<i>but she didn't want
any part of him.</i>

330
00:22:23,075 --> 00:22:26,534
You can't just barge into
this house any time you want.

331
00:22:26,679 --> 00:22:29,171
Why not? I pay the bills.

332
00:22:29,315 --> 00:22:32,046
Just get out of here, Frank.
I don't want to talk to you.

333
00:22:32,184 --> 00:22:33,345
Christine...

334
00:22:33,485 --> 00:22:35,564
<i>That's when Danny blocked
his father's way upstairs.</i>

335
00:22:35,588 --> 00:22:37,853
You heard what she
said, Dad. Get out of here!

336
00:22:37,990 --> 00:22:41,154
Get out of my way. This is
between your mother and me.

337
00:22:42,761 --> 00:22:45,458
Don't you ever lay your
hand on me like that again.

338
00:22:52,938 --> 00:22:55,338
<i>Danny didn't have to throw
him downstairs like that.</i>

339
00:22:55,407 --> 00:22:57,569
<i>Luckily, Frank wasn't hurt.</i>

340
00:22:57,710 --> 00:23:00,236
<i>I helped him up and
told him we should go.</i>

341
00:23:00,379 --> 00:23:03,042
<i>Did Frank Hayes make a
threat of any kind to his wife</i>

342
00:23:03,182 --> 00:23:04,980
<i>before you left?</i>

343
00:23:05,117 --> 00:23:06,278
<i>Yes, he did.</i>

344
00:23:06,418 --> 00:23:11,118
I'm not going to stand by while my
wife shares my bed with somebody else.

345
00:23:12,558 --> 00:23:16,120
I want you moved
out of this house

346
00:23:16,262 --> 00:23:18,822
by the time I come back
here tomorrow night.

347
00:23:18,964 --> 00:23:21,229
Or what, Frank?

348
00:23:21,367 --> 00:23:24,997
I'll kill you, that's what.

349
00:23:26,372 --> 00:23:31,834
Mr. Parker, do you believe
that Frank Hayes literally meant

350
00:23:31,977 --> 00:23:35,311
that he intended to return to
that house the following day

351
00:23:35,447 --> 00:23:37,439
and kill his wife Christine?

352
00:23:37,583 --> 00:23:39,882
Objection. Calls
for speculation.

353
00:23:40,019 --> 00:23:42,784
This is hardly
speculation, Your Honor.

354
00:23:42,922 --> 00:23:45,721
The witness had a lengthy
conversation with the victim

355
00:23:45,858 --> 00:23:47,121
the moment they left the house.

356
00:23:47,259 --> 00:23:49,524
- Then it's hearsay.
- Overruled.

357
00:23:49,662 --> 00:23:52,222
The witness may
answer that question.

358
00:23:52,364 --> 00:23:55,129
No, he wouldn't
have hurt Christine.

359
00:23:55,267 --> 00:23:59,034
In fact, when we got to the
car, he broke down in tears.

360
00:23:59,171 --> 00:24:00,969
He said he loved his Chris

361
00:24:01,106 --> 00:24:03,575
and prayed to God they'd
be able to work things out.

362
00:24:03,709 --> 00:24:05,041
I have nothing further.

363
00:24:08,514 --> 00:24:13,214
Uh, Mr. Parker, I apologize.

364
00:24:13,352 --> 00:24:17,687
I just can't quite remember
the name of the restaurant

365
00:24:17,823 --> 00:24:20,691
where you and Frank had
dinner the night before he died.

366
00:24:20,826 --> 00:24:22,317
Scully's Grill.

367
00:24:22,461 --> 00:24:26,523
Oh. I believe that's
Scully's Bar and Grill, isn't it?

368
00:24:26,665 --> 00:24:28,258
Yeah, actually I think it is.

369
00:24:28,400 --> 00:24:30,767
So, did you and
your friend Frank

370
00:24:30,903 --> 00:24:33,964
have some alcoholic
drinks along with dinner?

371
00:24:34,106 --> 00:24:35,938
- Yes, I believe we did.
- How many?

372
00:24:36,075 --> 00:24:38,340
- One or two.
- Or three or four?

373
00:24:38,477 --> 00:24:39,536
No more than three.

374
00:24:39,678 --> 00:24:44,514
Oh, so I guess you were feeling
no pain, maybe a little woozy.

375
00:24:44,650 --> 00:24:45,879
I was perfectly fine.

376
00:24:46,018 --> 00:24:49,955
Do you mean to tell me that
three strong alcoholic drinks

377
00:24:50,089 --> 00:24:53,890
won't impair your perception of
things the way it does everybody else?

378
00:24:54,026 --> 00:24:56,860
I remember that night
very clearly, Mr. Matlock.

379
00:24:59,965 --> 00:25:03,197
Let me ask you
something, Mr. Parker.

380
00:25:03,335 --> 00:25:09,002
You say that Frank was a
good and loving husband

381
00:25:09,141 --> 00:25:11,440
who never physically
abused his wife.

382
00:25:11,577 --> 00:25:13,409
That's right.

383
00:25:13,545 --> 00:25:18,313
Uh, how often did you spend
an evening with the two of them?

384
00:25:18,450 --> 00:25:19,577
Excuse me?

385
00:25:19,718 --> 00:25:21,584
Well, in all the years
that you've known them,

386
00:25:21,720 --> 00:25:24,155
how often would you
say you and your wife

387
00:25:24,289 --> 00:25:26,952
spent an evening with
Frank and Christine Hayes?

388
00:25:27,092 --> 00:25:28,151
I don't know.

389
00:25:28,293 --> 00:25:30,592
Would it be closer
to one or a hundred?

390
00:25:30,729 --> 00:25:32,288
Frank and I were buddies.

391
00:25:32,431 --> 00:25:34,366
We used to hang out
together, not with our wives.

392
00:25:34,500 --> 00:25:39,529
The truth is, you hardly spent
any time with the two of them,

393
00:25:39,671 --> 00:25:42,937
so you couldn't know whether he
was a good and loving husband.

394
00:25:43,075 --> 00:25:44,703
I knew Frank. You didn't.

395
00:25:46,211 --> 00:25:49,477
You knew what he drank.

396
00:25:49,615 --> 00:25:52,414
You knew what sports
he liked to bet on.

397
00:25:52,551 --> 00:25:55,316
You knew what cars
he wanted to buy.

398
00:25:55,454 --> 00:25:58,686
But you didn't have a
clue how he treated his wife

399
00:25:58,824 --> 00:26:01,259
or how he got
along with his son.

400
00:26:01,393 --> 00:26:02,656
That's not true.

401
00:26:02,795 --> 00:26:04,491
Mr. Parker...

402
00:26:06,098 --> 00:26:07,794
I'm sure you miss Frank.

403
00:26:07,933 --> 00:26:10,960
I believe he was
your best friend,

404
00:26:11,103 --> 00:26:16,303
and my guess is that
you'd feel a lot better

405
00:26:16,442 --> 00:26:18,673
if his death were avenged.

406
00:26:18,811 --> 00:26:22,373
Don't you dare
patronize me, damn it.

407
00:26:22,514 --> 00:26:23,709
Mr. Parker...

408
00:26:23,849 --> 00:26:25,760
Your Honor, he's trying
to twist everything around.

409
00:26:25,784 --> 00:26:27,377
Danny hated Frank.

410
00:26:27,519 --> 00:26:29,784
- Move to strike...
- He's glad he's dead.

411
00:26:29,922 --> 00:26:31,823
Hey, shut up! You
don't know anything.

412
00:26:31,957 --> 00:26:33,687
- Sit down, young man!
- He's lying!

413
00:26:33,826 --> 00:26:36,853
Mr. Matlock, restrain your client,
or I'll have the bailiff remove him.

414
00:27:04,123 --> 00:27:06,354
This is a beautiful garden.

415
00:27:09,528 --> 00:27:11,360
Rose, Danny asked me to come by

416
00:27:11,497 --> 00:27:14,160
and tell you that he loves you.

417
00:27:14,299 --> 00:27:16,200
And he's sorry.

418
00:27:19,271 --> 00:27:21,103
Danny says that
you were in the house

419
00:27:21,240 --> 00:27:24,039
the night Frank came
home with his friend.

420
00:27:24,176 --> 00:27:25,974
I was in my room.

421
00:27:26,111 --> 00:27:28,580
I didn't hear anything.

422
00:27:28,714 --> 00:27:30,546
I understand it got pretty loud.

423
00:27:30,682 --> 00:27:32,514
You didn't hear anything?

424
00:27:34,686 --> 00:27:36,348
My son is dead.

425
00:27:37,723 --> 00:27:39,521
Leave me alone.

426
00:27:39,658 --> 00:27:42,355
No one can bring
your son back, Rose.

427
00:27:43,929 --> 00:27:45,921
But you don't want
to lose Danny, too.

428
00:27:46,064 --> 00:27:49,296
If you'll just testify that you
heard Frank threaten Christine.

429
00:27:49,434 --> 00:27:51,403
I told you, I didn't
hear anything.

430
00:27:55,841 --> 00:27:59,209
Mr. Connolly, call
your next witness.

431
00:28:00,679 --> 00:28:03,376
The state calls Christine
Hayes to the stand,

432
00:28:03,515 --> 00:28:06,451
and asks that she be deemed a
hostile witness under the evidence code.

433
00:28:06,585 --> 00:28:08,281
It is so deemed.

434
00:28:08,420 --> 00:28:09,820
Mrs. Hayes...

435
00:28:11,190 --> 00:28:15,184
would your characterize your
husband as a violent or abusive man?

436
00:28:15,327 --> 00:28:16,920
Yes.

437
00:28:17,062 --> 00:28:18,792
Did he physically abuse you?

438
00:28:18,931 --> 00:28:20,923
Many times, yes.

439
00:28:21,066 --> 00:28:23,968
- Did you ever call the police?
- No.

440
00:28:25,237 --> 00:28:27,001
Ever file a charge against him?

441
00:28:27,139 --> 00:28:29,335
No.

442
00:28:29,474 --> 00:28:30,999
File for a divorce?

443
00:28:31,143 --> 00:28:32,736
We had separated.

444
00:28:34,479 --> 00:28:36,519
Mrs. Hayes, you want us
to believe that your husband

445
00:28:36,648 --> 00:28:39,812
was a dangerous and violent
man who chronically abused you,

446
00:28:39,952 --> 00:28:44,481
yet you never called the police
or filed a charge or divorced him.

447
00:28:44,623 --> 00:28:45,852
Why is that?

448
00:28:45,991 --> 00:28:48,688
Because he always
said that he was sorry...

449
00:28:49,995 --> 00:28:54,763
that he had been working too hard
or was tired or had too much to drink.

450
00:28:54,900 --> 00:28:57,495
He always said that it
would never happen again.

451
00:28:59,271 --> 00:29:02,298
And I always felt that someday,
if I just held on long enough,

452
00:29:02,441 --> 00:29:04,205
that he would change.

453
00:29:05,544 --> 00:29:07,172
So, I stood by him.

454
00:29:07,312 --> 00:29:09,076
- By having an affair?
- Objection.

455
00:29:09,214 --> 00:29:10,842
Withdraw the question.

456
00:29:10,983 --> 00:29:12,849
Mrs. Hayes...

457
00:29:14,152 --> 00:29:16,815
we've heard that your
husband threatened to kill you

458
00:29:16,955 --> 00:29:20,153
if he found you at home
at the evening of June 9th.

459
00:29:20,292 --> 00:29:21,726
Did you believe him?

460
00:29:21,860 --> 00:29:23,954
I was very scared.

461
00:29:24,096 --> 00:29:26,258
Then, of course, you
called the police this time?

462
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:29,366
No.

463
00:29:31,336 --> 00:29:33,635
You feared for your life,

464
00:29:33,772 --> 00:29:35,798
yet you didn't think that
your husband's threat

465
00:29:35,941 --> 00:29:38,240
warranted one call
to the police, did you?

466
00:29:41,046 --> 00:29:45,313
Tell us, where were you at the
time that your son shot your husband?

467
00:29:45,450 --> 00:29:47,976
- In the house.
- Doing what?

468
00:29:48,120 --> 00:29:50,555
Cooking? Vacuuming?

469
00:29:50,689 --> 00:29:52,180
On the phone?

470
00:29:52,324 --> 00:29:54,589
- With your boyfriend?
- Objection.

471
00:29:54,726 --> 00:29:56,592
You weren't scared
at all, were you?

472
00:29:56,728 --> 00:29:57,728
Mr. Connolly.

473
00:29:57,796 --> 00:29:59,321
You did nothing
to protect yourself

474
00:29:59,464 --> 00:30:01,476
because you didn't think that
your husband's threat was serious.

475
00:30:01,500 --> 00:30:05,028
Your son didn't think it was serious.
And, in fact, the threat was not serious.

476
00:30:05,170 --> 00:30:06,570
Mr. Connolly!

477
00:30:06,705 --> 00:30:09,368
No. No, that is not true.

478
00:30:09,508 --> 00:30:12,342
I'm done here.

479
00:30:19,718 --> 00:30:20,879
Mr. Matlock?

480
00:30:22,954 --> 00:30:26,186
No questions at this
time, Your Honor.

481
00:30:26,325 --> 00:30:28,055
The witness may step down.

482
00:30:28,193 --> 00:30:32,426
This appears a good
time to break for lunch.

483
00:30:32,564 --> 00:30:35,591
This court is in
recess until 2 P.M.

484
00:30:41,840 --> 00:30:43,968
It's better than being in jail.

485
00:30:44,109 --> 00:30:45,407
Yeah.

486
00:30:47,045 --> 00:30:50,447
Danny...

487
00:30:50,582 --> 00:30:53,313
I think I'm going to have
to put you on the stand.

488
00:30:56,555 --> 00:30:59,992
It's very risky.

489
00:31:00,125 --> 00:31:02,993
It opens you up to
a cross-examination.

490
00:31:03,128 --> 00:31:04,824
After we hear your
side of the story,

491
00:31:04,963 --> 00:31:07,308
then you're going to have to
answer Mr. Connolly's questions,

492
00:31:07,332 --> 00:31:11,099
and I have absolutely no control
over what he might ask you.

493
00:31:11,236 --> 00:31:13,034
He sure went after Mom.

494
00:31:13,171 --> 00:31:14,605
Yep.

495
00:31:14,740 --> 00:31:17,369
Just like he'll go after you.

496
00:31:17,509 --> 00:31:22,470
But you're the only witness who can
respond to Sam Parker's testimony.

497
00:31:22,614 --> 00:31:23,946
It was very damaging.

498
00:31:25,150 --> 00:31:26,550
So, what do I do?

499
00:31:26,685 --> 00:31:29,245
Well, I want you to
tell about the night

500
00:31:29,388 --> 00:31:31,414
your father brought
Sam Parker to the house.

501
00:31:31,556 --> 00:31:33,320
I want you to tell
what really happened,

502
00:31:33,458 --> 00:31:36,394
everything that was different
from what Sam Parker said.

503
00:31:36,528 --> 00:31:38,087
All right. I can do that.

504
00:31:39,364 --> 00:31:42,630
Mainly, I want you to
make the jury understand

505
00:31:42,768 --> 00:31:45,363
the fear you had
for your mother,

506
00:31:45,504 --> 00:31:49,168
and how certain you were that your father
would carry out his threat to kill her.

507
00:31:50,475 --> 00:31:51,909
OK.

508
00:31:52,043 --> 00:31:56,242
When Mr. Connolly questions
you, don't lose your temper.

509
00:31:58,183 --> 00:31:59,913
OK.

510
00:32:00,051 --> 00:32:01,691
You already lost it in
the courtroom once.

511
00:32:01,753 --> 00:32:03,244
This is going to be tougher.

512
00:32:03,388 --> 00:32:05,857
He's going to come at
you with everything he's got.

513
00:32:05,991 --> 00:32:07,951
He's going to bait you.
He's going to provoke you.

514
00:32:07,993 --> 00:32:11,088
He's going to try his damnedest
to make you lose your temper

515
00:32:11,229 --> 00:32:13,960
so the jury will see you
as a hot-tempered kid

516
00:32:14,099 --> 00:32:17,729
who hated his father and
couldn't wait to shoot him dead.

517
00:32:19,204 --> 00:32:21,400
I didn't want to shoot him.

518
00:32:24,142 --> 00:32:26,008
How can you sit
there and eat like that?

519
00:32:26,144 --> 00:32:27,942
Like nothing is happening?

520
00:32:30,182 --> 00:32:33,152
Like I told you, it's
lunch and I'm hungry.

521
00:32:33,285 --> 00:32:35,982
Now, see, that's what
I was talking about.

522
00:32:36,121 --> 00:32:38,784
Now you're mad at
me, and I'm on your side.

523
00:32:42,427 --> 00:32:44,521
All right. I'm sorry.

524
00:32:44,663 --> 00:32:49,897
Now, no matter what
Mr. Connolly says,

525
00:32:50,035 --> 00:32:52,027
don't argue with him.

526
00:32:52,170 --> 00:32:54,105
If there's any fighting
to do, let me do it.

527
00:32:55,340 --> 00:32:56,865
OK, I understand.

528
00:32:58,076 --> 00:32:59,601
Eat a little lunch.

529
00:33:01,112 --> 00:33:03,308
Come on, it will help
you keep calm. Eat.

530
00:33:05,217 --> 00:33:09,450
Danny, why did
you shoot your dad?

531
00:33:11,122 --> 00:33:14,559
If I had let him go into that house,
he would have killed my mother.

532
00:33:14,693 --> 00:33:16,821
How can you be
so certain of that?

533
00:33:16,962 --> 00:33:19,796
He told her that he would
kill her the night before.

534
00:33:19,931 --> 00:33:26,428
But your father had made
threats against your mother

535
00:33:26,571 --> 00:33:28,335
on other occasions, hadn't he?

536
00:33:30,308 --> 00:33:34,302
He would threaten to beat
her up... And then he would.

537
00:33:34,446 --> 00:33:37,746
But he had never threatened
to kill her before, no.

538
00:33:39,184 --> 00:33:42,154
Danny, you heard
Sam Parker's account

539
00:33:42,287 --> 00:33:45,155
of what took place that evening.

540
00:33:45,290 --> 00:33:46,986
Was it correct?

541
00:33:48,326 --> 00:33:49,326
No.

542
00:33:49,461 --> 00:33:51,487
In your own words, would
you give us your memory

543
00:33:51,630 --> 00:33:54,031
of what happened that night?

544
00:33:56,268 --> 00:34:00,000
I was in my room when my
father came home with Mr. Parker.

545
00:34:00,138 --> 00:34:03,802
I remember that he
called out for my mom.

546
00:34:06,611 --> 00:34:09,137
Come on, Sam, it's all right.

547
00:34:09,281 --> 00:34:10,579
Chris?

548
00:34:11,950 --> 00:34:13,475
It's Frank.

549
00:34:17,856 --> 00:34:20,257
Frank, what are you doing here?

550
00:34:21,660 --> 00:34:23,253
I've missed you, Chris.

551
00:34:24,462 --> 00:34:28,991
Come on, ask Sam here.
You're all I ever talk about.

552
00:34:29,134 --> 00:34:31,262
I don't remember that dress.

553
00:34:31,403 --> 00:34:32,769
Is everything OK?

554
00:34:32,904 --> 00:34:35,601
<i>When my dad saw
Mr. Williams, he lost it.</i>

555
00:34:35,740 --> 00:34:37,231
You whore.

556
00:34:37,375 --> 00:34:38,866
He's just a friend, Frank.

557
00:34:39,010 --> 00:34:41,172
You're the one who said
we should see other people.

558
00:34:41,313 --> 00:34:42,906
Get the hell out of my house!

559
00:34:43,048 --> 00:34:44,949
Go, go.

560
00:34:45,083 --> 00:34:47,814
OK, but I'll call you later.

561
00:34:49,554 --> 00:34:51,113
<i>As soon as Mr. Williams left,</i>

562
00:34:51,256 --> 00:34:53,225
<i>my dad started moving
towards my mother</i>

563
00:34:53,358 --> 00:34:56,328
<i>with this real scary
look in his eyes.</i>

564
00:34:59,831 --> 00:35:02,494
- Where do you think you're going?
- To bed.

565
00:35:02,634 --> 00:35:05,866
You've been
disloyal to me, Chris.

566
00:35:06,004 --> 00:35:07,495
You know my feelings about that.

567
00:35:07,639 --> 00:35:09,904
Please... Please,
Frank, just go.

568
00:35:10,041 --> 00:35:14,672
No, not till we have a
little talk in the bedroom.

569
00:35:14,813 --> 00:35:16,611
Look, Dad, come on.
She asked you to go.

570
00:35:16,748 --> 00:35:18,876
Just don't cause
any trouble, OK?

571
00:35:20,552 --> 00:35:23,818
Don't you ever put your
hand on me like that again.

572
00:35:23,955 --> 00:35:26,754
<i>My dad turned around
and took a swing at me.</i>

573
00:35:26,891 --> 00:35:30,328
<i>I ducked, and I guess
he just lost his balance.</i>

574
00:35:34,199 --> 00:35:36,759
<i>That's when my father
looked up at my mother</i>

575
00:35:36,901 --> 00:35:40,838
<i>with this look I'd
never seen before.</i>

576
00:35:40,972 --> 00:35:43,840
You're not my wife.

577
00:35:43,975 --> 00:35:48,276
You're a filthy slut.

578
00:35:48,413 --> 00:35:52,214
I'm going to make you pay
for bringing men into this house.

579
00:35:52,350 --> 00:35:54,182
Leave. Just leave.

580
00:35:54,319 --> 00:35:56,811
Oh, I'm going.

581
00:35:56,955 --> 00:35:59,982
But I'm coming
back tomorrow night.

582
00:36:00,125 --> 00:36:05,120
And if every piece of your
clothing isn't out of this house,

583
00:36:05,263 --> 00:36:07,425
I'll kill you.

584
00:36:09,567 --> 00:36:13,163
Did you call the police after your
father left the house that night?

585
00:36:13,304 --> 00:36:14,863
Yes, I did.

586
00:36:15,006 --> 00:36:17,771
My mom begged me
not to, but I did anyway.

587
00:36:17,909 --> 00:36:19,377
What did you say to them?

588
00:36:19,511 --> 00:36:23,915
I told them that my dad said
that he was going to kill my mom.

589
00:36:24,049 --> 00:36:26,527
The police said that there was
nothing they could do about that

590
00:36:26,551 --> 00:36:28,349
unless a crime was committed.

591
00:36:28,486 --> 00:36:29,977
So, I hung up.

592
00:36:30,121 --> 00:36:31,316
Did you leave your name?

593
00:36:32,991 --> 00:36:34,289
No.

594
00:36:38,063 --> 00:36:39,998
That might have helped.

595
00:36:41,800 --> 00:36:45,134
Why didn't you try to get
your mother out of the house

596
00:36:45,270 --> 00:36:47,205
before your father came back?

597
00:36:47,338 --> 00:36:49,637
I did. I tried.

598
00:36:49,774 --> 00:36:52,710
But she said the reason
she separated from my dad

599
00:36:52,844 --> 00:36:55,056
was so that she wouldn't have
to be afraid of him anymore.

600
00:36:55,080 --> 00:36:56,378
She didn't want to run.

601
00:36:56,514 --> 00:36:58,312
Ah.

602
00:36:58,450 --> 00:37:01,284
It sounds like...

603
00:37:01,419 --> 00:37:06,323
your mother was less afraid of
your father's threats than you were.

604
00:37:09,694 --> 00:37:13,392
My mom always thought that my
dad had beaten her for the last time.

605
00:37:15,800 --> 00:37:17,928
She was always wrong.

606
00:37:18,069 --> 00:37:21,005
Danny, are you
absolutely certain,

607
00:37:21,139 --> 00:37:24,166
if you had not shot your father,

608
00:37:24,309 --> 00:37:26,210
he would have
killed your mother?

609
00:37:27,512 --> 00:37:31,108
I have never been more
positive about anything in my life.

610
00:37:33,084 --> 00:37:35,019
No further questions.

611
00:37:36,788 --> 00:37:38,381
Mr. Connolly?

612
00:37:43,228 --> 00:37:47,898
Danny... what kind of
dad was your father?

613
00:37:49,167 --> 00:37:52,501
He was... He was OK sometimes.

614
00:37:52,637 --> 00:37:53,832
Sometimes, not.

615
00:37:53,972 --> 00:37:56,635
Did he ever help you
with your homework?

616
00:37:56,775 --> 00:37:59,745
Did he ever come out to
see you play Little League?

617
00:37:59,878 --> 00:38:01,813
Did he ever take you
to the Braves games?

618
00:38:01,946 --> 00:38:05,041
No, not very often.
He worked a lot.

619
00:38:06,217 --> 00:38:07,776
All those years,

620
00:38:07,919 --> 00:38:11,549
watching your friends playing catch
with their dads on their front lawns...

621
00:38:11,689 --> 00:38:14,090
You must have
resented him terribly.

622
00:38:15,293 --> 00:38:17,922
I didn't resent my dad.

623
00:38:19,798 --> 00:38:21,994
Did you ever see your
father mistreat your mother?

624
00:38:22,133 --> 00:38:23,396
Yes.

625
00:38:23,535 --> 00:38:26,095
And that resentment turned
to anger, then hatred, didn't it?

626
00:38:27,772 --> 00:38:31,732
I was angry, but I
didn't hate my father.

627
00:38:34,879 --> 00:38:37,713
The night your father came
home with Sam Parker...

628
00:38:39,450 --> 00:38:43,319
he didn't trip down those
stairs. You pulled him.

629
00:38:43,454 --> 00:38:45,446
You threw him down, didn't you?

630
00:38:46,658 --> 00:38:49,628
- I didn't mean to...
- Yes or no?

631
00:38:49,761 --> 00:38:51,161
- Yes, but...
- Had he hit you?

632
00:38:51,296 --> 00:38:53,629
- No, but he...
- He didn't hit you.

633
00:38:53,765 --> 00:38:55,927
And you didn't call the
police, did you, Danny?

634
00:38:56,067 --> 00:39:00,232
That's why they didn't have your
name, because you didn't call the police.

635
00:39:00,371 --> 00:39:01,703
Yes, I did.

636
00:39:01,840 --> 00:39:03,918
You knew your father wasn't
going to kill your mother.

637
00:39:03,942 --> 00:39:05,274
He said that he would!

638
00:39:05,410 --> 00:39:07,379
He was drunk, and he was upset

639
00:39:07,512 --> 00:39:10,641
at seeing another man
in his house with his wife.

640
00:39:10,782 --> 00:39:12,478
It was an idle threat.

641
00:39:12,617 --> 00:39:14,108
He knew it. Your mother knew it.

642
00:39:14,252 --> 00:39:16,380
- And you knew it, didn't you?
- No!

643
00:39:16,521 --> 00:39:18,080
Oh, come on, Danny.

644
00:39:18,223 --> 00:39:20,351
The police never
found any knife or gun

645
00:39:20,491 --> 00:39:22,426
anywhere in your
father's possession.

646
00:39:22,560 --> 00:39:24,672
- He would have strangled her!
- Admit it, you hated him.

647
00:39:24,696 --> 00:39:26,426
You don't know
what I had to live with!

648
00:39:26,564 --> 00:39:28,795
- You're glad he's dead, aren't you?
- Objection.

649
00:39:28,933 --> 00:39:30,526
- Aren't you?
- Objection!

650
00:39:30,668 --> 00:39:32,102
No further questions.

651
00:39:58,463 --> 00:40:00,762
Thank you for seeing me, Rose.

652
00:40:01,933 --> 00:40:04,129
You sounded
worried on the phone.

653
00:40:04,269 --> 00:40:06,067
Now that you're
here, you look worried.

654
00:40:06,204 --> 00:40:07,433
I am.

655
00:40:07,572 --> 00:40:09,332
I'd like to tell you
everything will be okay,

656
00:40:09,440 --> 00:40:10,965
but the fact is...

657
00:40:12,710 --> 00:40:14,906
There's a good possibility...

658
00:40:15,046 --> 00:40:16,957
You think Danny's going
to be convicted, don't you?

659
00:40:16,981 --> 00:40:19,450
Ben doesn't think the
jury believed his testimony.

660
00:40:19,584 --> 00:40:20,584
Why?

661
00:40:20,685 --> 00:40:23,814
Ben's been reading
jurors' faces for 35 years.

662
00:40:23,955 --> 00:40:26,035
He knows when they're not
buying a defendant's story.

663
00:40:26,157 --> 00:40:28,456
What about my testimony?

664
00:40:28,593 --> 00:40:29,856
Danny is your son.

665
00:40:29,994 --> 00:40:31,963
The jury expects
you to protect him.

666
00:40:33,231 --> 00:40:35,166
How long a sentence
could he get?

667
00:40:35,300 --> 00:40:37,860
He's being tried as
an adult, Christine.

668
00:40:38,002 --> 00:40:41,735
He could get... Life.

669
00:40:55,386 --> 00:40:58,481
The defense calls
Rose Hayes to the stand.

670
00:41:01,893 --> 00:41:06,194
Mrs. Hayes, I want to thank you.

671
00:41:07,765 --> 00:41:10,792
I know you loved your
son Frank very much,

672
00:41:10,935 --> 00:41:15,202
and I understand how
difficult this must be for you.

673
00:41:15,340 --> 00:41:18,777
How many years did you
live with your son Frank,

674
00:41:18,910 --> 00:41:22,472
your daughter-in-law Christine
and your grandson Danny?

675
00:41:22,613 --> 00:41:24,912
Four years, last month.

676
00:41:25,049 --> 00:41:27,143
And in those four years,

677
00:41:27,285 --> 00:41:33,418
did you ever see or hear your son
and your daughter-in-law fighting?

678
00:41:35,159 --> 00:41:38,095
Well, sometimes at night,

679
00:41:38,229 --> 00:41:40,357
when I was in my room...

680
00:41:41,766 --> 00:41:43,166
I heard them fighting.

681
00:41:43,301 --> 00:41:49,172
Did you ever see your son
hit your daughter-in-law?

682
00:41:49,307 --> 00:41:51,003
No.

683
00:41:51,142 --> 00:41:55,876
Did Danny ever tell you that
he saw his father hit his mother?

684
00:42:00,918 --> 00:42:02,352
Yes.

685
00:42:06,224 --> 00:42:10,059
Mr. Matlock, does your
witness need a recess?

686
00:42:11,829 --> 00:42:13,058
Rose?

687
00:42:13,197 --> 00:42:16,258
No, thank you, Judge.

688
00:42:18,503 --> 00:42:23,100
When Danny told you that he had
seen his father hitting his mother,

689
00:42:23,241 --> 00:42:24,607
did you believe him?

690
00:42:24,742 --> 00:42:28,474
My son works very hard.

691
00:42:28,613 --> 00:42:30,844
He's under a lot of pressure.

692
00:42:30,982 --> 00:42:35,113
I understand. Did
Christine ever tell you

693
00:42:35,253 --> 00:42:37,722
that her husband
was hitting her?

694
00:42:40,825 --> 00:42:42,316
She...

695
00:42:44,429 --> 00:42:46,762
She always said
it was her fault.

696
00:42:46,898 --> 00:42:49,697
Mrs. Hayes, were
you in the house

697
00:42:49,834 --> 00:42:53,737
the night that your son brought
Sam Parker home with him?

698
00:42:53,871 --> 00:42:55,567
I was in the kitchen.

699
00:42:55,706 --> 00:43:00,610
Mrs. Hayes, there have been
two entirely different stories told

700
00:43:00,745 --> 00:43:02,839
about what took
place that night.

701
00:43:02,980 --> 00:43:07,816
I'd like you to give us your
memory of what you saw and heard.

702
00:43:07,952 --> 00:43:11,081
Now, you were in the kitchen

703
00:43:11,222 --> 00:43:14,056
when you heard your son's voice?

704
00:43:14,192 --> 00:43:15,353
Yes.

705
00:43:15,493 --> 00:43:17,860
But you didn't go
in and say hello?

706
00:43:17,995 --> 00:43:20,089
He was very angry.

707
00:43:20,231 --> 00:43:25,101
Mrs. Hayes, in all the years
you have known your son,

708
00:43:25,236 --> 00:43:28,934
how many times have
you seen him that angry?

709
00:43:29,073 --> 00:43:30,666
Oh.

710
00:43:30,808 --> 00:43:34,506
My Frank was like his father.

711
00:43:37,048 --> 00:43:41,418
Joe worked very hard,
and I know he loved me...

712
00:43:43,354 --> 00:43:45,323
but sometimes...

713
00:43:47,258 --> 00:43:49,454
he had a temper.

714
00:43:51,062 --> 00:43:54,191
Your husband hit you?

715
00:43:55,633 --> 00:43:58,626
He was always sorry.

716
00:43:58,769 --> 00:44:02,831
And deep down,
he was a good man.

717
00:44:04,008 --> 00:44:05,636
Just like Frank?

718
00:44:07,345 --> 00:44:09,644
Yes. Deep down.

719
00:44:09,780 --> 00:44:11,271
I see.

720
00:44:12,850 --> 00:44:16,287
You haven't had it
easy, Mrs. Hayes.

721
00:44:16,420 --> 00:44:19,788
But we must have an answer.

722
00:44:19,924 --> 00:44:23,088
How many times
have you seen your son

723
00:44:23,227 --> 00:44:27,562
as angry as he was the night he
brought Sam Parker to the house?

724
00:44:31,135 --> 00:44:32,603
Never.

725
00:44:35,573 --> 00:44:38,975
I'm almost finished,
Rose, I promise.

726
00:44:40,545 --> 00:44:44,175
Did you hear your son
threaten to return the next day

727
00:44:44,315 --> 00:44:48,776
and kill his wife if
she were still there?

728
00:44:51,589 --> 00:44:53,353
Yes.

729
00:44:55,293 --> 00:44:59,890
Were you afraid for Christine?

730
00:45:16,647 --> 00:45:18,172
Nothing further.

731
00:45:27,024 --> 00:45:30,552
I know that it's wrong to kill,

732
00:45:30,695 --> 00:45:33,529
even someone like him.

733
00:45:33,664 --> 00:45:37,260
But what if I hadn't,
and you were dead?

734
00:45:40,905 --> 00:45:43,465
I had no choice, did I?

735
00:45:43,608 --> 00:45:48,637
Oh, Danny, the fault is mine.

736
00:45:48,779 --> 00:45:51,874
I should have reported
the abuse to the police.

737
00:45:52,016 --> 00:45:55,077
I should have divorced
your father years ago.

738
00:45:57,555 --> 00:45:59,285
I should have gone with you

739
00:45:59,423 --> 00:46:01,324
the night you wanted
to take me away.

740
00:46:03,327 --> 00:46:05,057
The jury's coming in.

741
00:46:10,201 --> 00:46:14,605
Ladies and gentlemen of the
jury, have you reached a verdict?

742
00:46:16,274 --> 00:46:17,936
We have, Your Honor.

743
00:46:19,310 --> 00:46:21,609
On the charge of
murder in the first degree,

744
00:46:21,746 --> 00:46:25,513
we find the defendant
Daniel Hayes not guilty.

745
00:46:27,018 --> 00:46:29,044
Thank you, ladies and
gentlemen of the jury.

746
00:46:29,186 --> 00:46:30,381
You're dismissed.

747
00:46:30,521 --> 00:46:32,080
This court is adjourned.

748
00:46:33,958 --> 00:46:35,824
I'm so sorry.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
